{
  "description": "#3870 follow-up — the `blockType` and `tagId` DISPATCH BRANCHES of `list_blocks`, paginated. `query_list_blocks_pagination` drives only the `parentId` branch, whose helper (`pagination::list_children`) keysets on `(COALESCE(position, ?N), id)`. That is NOT the order the other branches use: `list_by_type` and `list_by_tag` are `ORDER BY id ASC` / `ORDER BY bt.block_id ASC`, and `list_agenda` / `list_agenda_range` key on `b.id` too. A mock that applies the `(position, id)` keyset to all of them resumes every page from a column the backend never ordered on, and — per #3821 — that changes WHICH rows a page holds, not merely their order. This fixture makes the difference maximal rather than incidental: the six content blocks are seeded with positions ANTI-CORRELATED to their ids (`p1` holds the greatest id, `p6` the least), so id order and position order are exact reverses and the recorded pages are DISJOINT from the pages a position keyset would return. There is no overlap to accidentally agree on, and a single-page step would not show it at all. `total_count` is pinned by the `blockType` steps: that arm is the one place `list_blocks_inner` overwrites `build_page_response`'s `None` with `count_blocks_by_type`, so a helper that hard-codes `null` fails here. The tag steps leave `p6` untagged so the tag result is a strict subset of the type result — a `tagId` the mock ignored would answer six rows where five are recorded. #3942 review note 7 added the `dateRange` (`list_agenda_range`) branch's own two-page pair: it is the ONE branch whose `Cursor` reuses the `deleted_at` slot for something that is not a tombstone (`ac.date`, via `Cursor::for_id_and_deleted_at`), and before this addition no fixture paginated it, so the `cursor` assertion never covered that slot mapping.",
